{
 "lead": "A neural network learns by passing numbers forward and comparing its output with a target. The activation function decides what each unit passes on; the loss function measures how wrong the output is. Both must be differentiable at almost all points (or have a usable subgradient), because gradient descent needs a derivative where it is applied; a few functions are differentiable only piecewise. This chapter gives the common activation functions—step, sigmoid, tanh, ReLU, leaky ReLU and softmax—and the common losses: squared error, binary cross-entropy, categorical cross-entropy and hinge. The same ideas appear in spam filters, UPI fraud detection and image classifiers.",
 "prereq": "You need: <a href=\"#multilayer-feedforward-networks-and-hidden-layers\">Chapter 5.3</a> for where these functions sit in a network, and Unit 2 for the basic idea of a loss function.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Activation functions decide the output of a unit",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A unit first computes a weighted sum z = Σ wᵢxᵢ + b, where wᵢ are weights, xᵢ are inputs, and b is the bias. The activation function f turns z into output a = f(z). It decides what the unit passes to the next layer. Training by gradient descent needs f to have a derivative, because <a href=\"#backpropagation-training\">backpropagation</a> multiplies these derivatives."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The step function gives 1 when z ≥ 0 and 0 otherwise. It matches the McCulloch–Pitts neuron from <a href=\"#biological-neuron-and-the-mcculloch-pitts-model\">Chapter 5.1</a>. Its derivative is zero almost everywhere, so a network built only from steps cannot learn hidden weights by gradients."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "σ(z) = 1/(1 + e⁻ᶻ)",
     "where": "z is the weighted input; e ≈ 2.718; σ(z) lies in (0,1). Derivative: σ(z)(1 − σ(z)), largest 0.25 at z = 0."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Sigmoid is used at an output unit for a yes/no probability, such as whether a transaction is fraud. For large positive or negative z the curve is flat; this is saturation, and the derivative becomes tiny."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "tanh(z) = (eᶻ − e⁻ᶻ)/(eᶻ + e⁻ᶻ)",
     "where": "range is (−1,1); derivative is 1 − tanh²(z). It is zero-centred, so hidden units train better than with sigmoid in shallow networks."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Tanh also saturates at both ends. For large |z| its derivative goes to zero, so deep networks with tanh hidden units learn slowly."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "ReLU, leaky ReLU and softmax",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "ReLU (rectified linear unit) is f(z) = max(0, z). For z > 0 the output is z; for z < 0 the output is 0. Its derivative is 1 for z > 0 and 0 for z < 0. At exactly z = 0 it is not differentiable; code picks 0 or 1. ReLU is cheap and does not saturate for positive z, so it is the default in hidden layers of deep networks."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "f(z) = max(0, z)",
     "where": "z is the weighted input; derivative is 1 if z > 0 and 0 if z < 0."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Leaky ReLU is f(z) = max(αz, z), where α is a small slope such as 0.01. On the negative side the output is αz, so the derivative is α instead of 0. This keeps a small gradient flowing."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "For K classes, softmax is yᵢ = e^{zᵢ} / Σⱼ e^{zⱼ}, where zᵢ is the logit (raw score) for class i and j runs over all K classes. Every yᵢ is positive and Σ yᵢ = 1, so the outputs act as class probabilities. Softmax sits at the output layer of a multiclass classifier, not in hidden layers."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Loss functions measure how wrong the output is",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Loss L compares prediction y with target t. Regression uses squared error: L = ½(y − t)². Its derivative with respect to y is y − t. It punishes large errors strongly."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Binary classification with a sigmoid output uses binary cross-entropy: L = −[t log y + (1 − t) log(1 − y)]. Here t is 0 or 1 and y is the predicted probability. If t = 1 and y is near 0, the loss is large."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Multiclass classification with softmax uses categorical cross-entropy: L = −Σᵢ tᵢ log yᵢ. Here t is a one-hot vector with 1 for the true class and 0 elsewhere. Only the true-class term survives, but write the sum over all classes."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Hinge loss is L = max(0, 1 − t y), where t is ±1 and y is the raw score. It is used in support vector machines (Unit 3)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The derivative matters: backpropagation gives ∂L/∂w, and optimizers use it to update weights. A zero derivative from saturation stops learning."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
